# Podium — every environment variable the binaries read, plus the ones the compose files
# interpolate. Copy to .env and fill in what you need, or run `podium-server init`, which
# writes a .env with fresh credentials already in it.
#
# This file is checked by a test: `go test ./deploy/...` fails if the source reads a
# PODIUM_* variable that is not listed here, or if this file documents one that no longer
# exists. Every line below is either a real variable or a real omission.
#
# Values shown after `=` are the defaults. A commented-out line means "this is the default,
# uncomment to change it". A line with no value that is NOT commented out is required BY A
# BINARY YOU RUN YOURSELF — there are seven, and docker-compose.yml supplies every one of
# them, so on the compose path they are not your problem.
#
# WHICH OF THESE YOU ACTUALLY HAVE TO SET, in four tiers. README.md has the same thing with
# the consequences spelled out: ./README.md#what-you-have-to-configure
#
#   1. To START, on the compose path: nothing. Every variable docker-compose.yml interpolates
#      has a default, and a test fails the build if that stops being true.
#
#   2. To make a FEATURE work, one variable each. Without it that one thing is off and
#      nothing else notices:
#        PODIUM_MEMORY_LLM_API_KEY        the shared memory (hindsight exits without it)
#        PODIUM_NODE_ENROLL_TOKEN         a worker's first run, single use
#        PODIUM_AGENT_SLACK_*_TOKEN       the Slack source — both or neither
#        PODIUM_AGENT_LINEAR_API_KEY      the Linear source
#        TS_AUTHKEY + PODIUM_TAILNET      the tailnet transport
#
#   3. CREDENTIALS THAT HAVE DEFAULTS, so nothing forces you to choose. Replace them before
#      anything you would miss. PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN is the only one that leaves the compose
#      network; PODIUM_PG_PASSWORD must be set BEFORE the first `up`, because it is baked
#      into the Postgres volume when it is initialised:
#        PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN  PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_API_KEY  PODIUM_PG_PASSWORD
#        PODIUM_S3_SECRET_KEY  PODIUM_AGENT_TOKEN
#
#   4. EVERYTHING ELSE IS CONFIG: ports, intervals, models, poll rates, labels, base URLs.
#      Working defaults, all of them. PODIUM_IMAGE_TAG is the one to pin regardless.
#
# The local transport has exactly one secret. The server, the worker, the conductor and the
# CLI all present PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN, and every one of them reads it from that single line.
#
# Running the binaries by hand instead? `podium-server init` writes the credentials with
# fresh random values and a master key beside them, and `set -a; . deploy/.env; set +a`
# configures your shell's CLI from the same file.

# =============================================================================
# podium-server — the control plane
# =============================================================================

# The Postgres DSN. The server migrates the schema on start. REQUIRED when you run the
# binary by hand; the compose files and deploy/run-host.sh both build it from
# PODIUM_PG_PASSWORD instead, so under `make stack-up` you can leave this empty.
PODIUM_DATABASE_URL=

# How clients and nodes reach the server.
#   local    loopback HTTP with a shared bearer token. One machine. The tested path.
#   tailnet  the server embeds its own Tailscale device (tsnet) and serves HTTPS on its
#            MagicDNS name. Workers can be anywhere on your tailnet.
#   host     like tailnet, but over the machine's existing tailscaled. Unverified.
#
# A fourth *deployment*, `podium-server init --transport host`, is not a PODIUM_TRANSPORT
# value. It still runs local (the shared token) and binds this machine's interfaces so
# workers reach it over a network you already have — WireGuard, a corporate VPN, a LAN.
# docker-compose.host.yml. See docs/networking.md#host-network-bring-your-own-routing.
#PODIUM_TRANSPORT=local

# local transport only. Must resolve to loopback or the server refuses to start — that
# token is the only credential there is, so it may not be exposed off the machine.
#PODIUM_LOCAL_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8080

# Waives the rule above. Two cases, and only those:
#   - a container, where loopback is the container's own and the published port is the
#     boundary (docker-compose.yml sets it for exactly that reason);
#   - a host-network deployment, where the boundary is a network you already trust
#     (WireGuard, a corporate VPN, a LAN). docker-compose.host.yml, or `make stack-up`
#     with PODIUM_LOCAL_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8080.
# On a public address this publishes the whole API behind one static token. The server
# logs a warning naming this variable on every start.
#PODIUM_LOCAL_ALLOW_UNSAFE_LISTEN=false

# REQUIRED for PODIUM_TRANSPORT=local. The shared bearer token every API call and the web UI
# present. There is no per-user identity under this transport and no RBAC anywhere: whoever
# holds this token can do everything.
PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN=

# The file holding the 32-byte AES-256 key every stored secret is encrypted under.
# Mode 0600 or 0400 — the server refuses to start on anything a group or other can read.
# Generate one with `podium-server gen-master-key --out master.key`.
# Leaving it unset is a supported configuration: the server starts, and every secret
# operation answers FailedPrecondition. Podium is still a task runner without secrets.
# THERE IS NO RECOVERY PATH. Back this file up somewhere that is not the control plane.
#PODIUM_MASTER_KEY_FILE=/etc/podium/master.key

# The same key inline, hex encoded. Development only: an environment variable is visible in
# /proc and in `docker inspect`. The server warns loudly when it is used, and the file wins
# if both are set.
#PODIUM_MASTER_KEY=

# --- Tailscale (PODIUM_TRANSPORT=tailnet or host) -----------------------------------------

# The Tailscale auth key, read on the FIRST RUN ONLY: after that PODIUM_TS_STATE_DIR is the
# device identity. Must be Reusable and Pre-approved and tagged tag:podium-server.
# This is Tailscale's key. It is NOT the Podium enrollment token below.
#TS_AUTHKEY=

# The Tailscale device name, and therefore the first label of the MagicDNS name: the server
# ends up at https://<this>.<your-tailnet>.ts.net.
#PODIUM_TS_HOSTNAME=podium

# Where tsnet keeps the device's node key. MUST PERSIST across restarts, or the server
# registers a new Tailscale device every time and the MagicDNS name drifts to podium-1,
# podium-2, ... In compose this is a named volume.
#PODIUM_TS_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/podium/tsnet

# The ACL tag a Tailscale device must carry before the server will let it enroll as a node.
#PODIUM_TS_REQUIRED_NODE_TAG=tag:podium-node

# Let an UNTAGGED tailnet device enroll as a node. This removes the network-level proof that
# a caller is an authorised worker and exists only for a tailnet with no ACL tags yet. The
# server warns loudly at startup when it is on.
#PODIUM_TS_ALLOW_UNTAGGED_NODES=false

# --- Object store (artifacts and rolled-up logs) ------------------------------------------
# Leaving PODIUM_S3_ENDPOINT empty disables artifacts entirely, which is supported: a task
# does not need artifacts to run. Nodes never talk to the object store — an artifact travels
# node -> server -> S3 — so no worker ever holds these credentials.

# host:port, or a full URL. A URL's scheme decides TLS; a bare host:port is plain HTTP
# unless PODIUM_S3_USE_SSL says otherwise.
#PODIUM_S3_ENDPOINT=

# The bucket. Created on start if it is missing.
#PODIUM_S3_BUCKET=podium

#PODIUM_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#PODIUM_S3_SECRET_KEY=

# The bundled object store ignores the region; a real S3 does not.
#PODIUM_S3_REGION=us-east-1

# Only consulted when PODIUM_S3_ENDPOINT is a bare host:port with no scheme.
#PODIUM_S3_USE_SSL=false

# Where the dev object store's API port is published on the host, read only by
# deploy/docker-compose.dev.yml. The production compose deliberately publishes no API port:
# there the server sits on the compose network beside the store, while in development it is
# a binary on the host that has to reach it over loopback. Move it when something else owns
# 9000, and match PODIUM_S3_ENDPOINT to it.
#PODIUM_S3_PORT=9000

# --- Log roll-up ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A finished task's log chunks move out of Postgres into the object store and are pruned
# from the hot table a grace period later. With no object store configured nothing is rolled
# up and nothing is pruned: the chunks stay in Postgres forever.

# How often the roll-up sweep runs.
#PODIUM_LOG_ROLLUP_INTERVAL=1m

# How long after a task reaches a terminal status before its log is eligible. It exists so a
# straggling event batch is not left out of the archive.
#PODIUM_LOG_ROLLUP_SETTLE=30s

# How often rolled-up chunks are deleted from Postgres.
#PODIUM_LOG_PRUNE_INTERVAL=1h

# How long a rolled-up task's chunks survive in Postgres before the prune takes them. This
# is your "logs stay fast for N" knob. An unparseable value is ignored rather than treated
# as zero: a typo must not silently delete a day of retention.
#PODIUM_LOG_CHUNK_GRACE=24h

# =============================================================================
# podium-node — the worker daemon
# =============================================================================
# Every one of these can also live in /etc/podium/node.yaml (the key is the name below,
# lowercased, without the PODIUM_NODE_ prefix). The environment wins over the file.

# The control plane's base URL. http:// for the local transport, https:// for tailnet/host —
# the daemon refuses a mismatch rather than failing later.
#PODIUM_NODE_SERVER=http://127.0.0.1:8080

#PODIUM_NODE_TRANSPORT=local

# REQUIRED for PODIUM_NODE_TRANSPORT=local: the server's PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN.
#PODIUM_NODE_LOCAL_TOKEN=

# Podium's own enrollment token, from `podium node enroll-token`. SINGLE USE, 1 hour default
# TTL, needed on the first run only — after that <data_dir>/identity.json is the identity.
# Not the same thing as TS_AUTHKEY.
#PODIUM_NODE_ENROLL_TOKEN=

# Holds identity.json (the node key, which the server returns exactly once and cannot
# reissue), each task's state, and the node's own tsnet state. Created if missing, and
# write-probed at startup. Put it on a fast local disk: it is where a task's workspace
# staging and secret files go.
#PODIUM_NODE_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/podium-node

# Comma separated. A task is only placed on a node whose labels are a superset of the
# task's, so these are how `--label linux/amd64` finds the right machine.
#PODIUM_NODE_LABELS=

# The concurrency budget the scheduler assigns against. 0 is refused, not silently accepted.
#PODIUM_NODE_MAX_TASKS=4

# /healthz, /readyz and /metrics. Loopback by default: none of it is authenticated.
#PODIUM_NODE_METRICS_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:9091

# Overrides the Docker engine endpoint. Empty means the usual DOCKER_HOST / docker context /
# default socket resolution. The data dir must be on the same host as the engine.
#PODIUM_NODE_DOCKER_HOST=

# The Tailscale auth key for this worker: reusable, pre-approved, tagged tag:podium-node.
# TS_AUTHKEY is honoured as a fallback; this prefixed name wins when both are set.
#PODIUM_NODE_TS_AUTHKEY=

# The worker's Tailscale device name. Empty derives it from the machine's hostname.
#PODIUM_NODE_TS_HOSTNAME=

# Turn on the LRU image-cache prune. OFF BY DEFAULT and deliberately so: a node shares its
# Docker engine with everything else on the machine. Even when it is on, Podium only ever
# removes an image it pulled itself — <data_dir>/images.json is the allow-list — and it
# never runs `docker system prune` or any bulk removal.
#PODIUM_NODE_IMAGE_CACHE_PRUNE=false

# The disk-usage fraction above which pruning starts. With pruning off, crossing it instead
# makes the node advertise free_slots=0: a machine that cannot fit another image cannot
# reliably start another task.
#PODIUM_NODE_IMAGE_CACHE_HIGH_WATERMARK=0.80

# Exit 0 once the control plane has drained this node and its last task has finished. This
# is the upgrade path: a supervisor restarts the process on the new binary. Without it a
# drained node stays connected and idle, which is what taking a machine out of service for
# maintenance wants.
#PODIUM_NODE_EXIT_ON_DRAIN=false

# Honour a task spec's `privileged: true` on a sidecar, which is what a docker-in-docker
# daemon beside a task needs. OFF BY DEFAULT and deliberately so: such a container is root on
# this machine's kernel, and none of the sandbox every other task runs under applies to it.
# Turn it on only on a node dedicated to that work, pair it with PODIUM_NODE_LABELS so only
# the specs that ask for the label land there, and read docs/security.md first.
#PODIUM_NODE_ALLOW_PRIVILEGED_SIDECARS=false

# =============================================================================
# podium-agent — the conductor
# =============================================================================
# A second long-lived process beside podium-server. It holds the bot's Slack identity, turns
# a mention into one Podium task running the agent runtime image, and relays what the agent
# says back into the thread. It is an ordinary API client of the control plane: it never
# opens the server's database, never sees the master key and never touches Docker.
# See docs/agent.md.

# REQUIRED. The Podium API base URL the conductor talks to. http:// for the local transport,
# https://podium.<tailnet>.ts.net for a tailnet.
PODIUM_AGENT_SERVER=

# REQUIRED when PODIUM_AGENT_SERVER is http://: the server's PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN. A tailnet
# control plane needs none — Tailscale's WhoIs names the caller.
PODIUM_AGENT_API_TOKEN=

# REQUIRED. The conductor's OWN database, podium_agent, on the same Postgres as the
# server's. It is a different database, not a schema in the same one, and the conductor
# migrates it on start. deploy/postgres/init.sql creates it on a fresh volume; for an
# existing install:
#   docker compose exec -T postgres createdb -U podium podium_agent
PODIUM_AGENT_DATABASE_URL=

# The Connect API, /healthz, /readyz and /metrics. Loopback by default and it should stay
# that way: PODIUM_AGENT_TOKEN is the only credential, and podium-server is what a browser
# reaches the AgentService through.
#PODIUM_AGENT_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8090

# REQUIRED. The bearer podium-server presents on proxied AgentService calls, and the only
# thing guarding every session the bot has had. As sensitive as PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN.
PODIUM_AGENT_TOKEN=

# Where profile.yaml, playbooks/ and prompts/ live. A playbook file is the ONLY boundary around
# what a turn can do and which secrets it gets: keep `secrets:` minimal per playbook.
#
# deploy/run-host.sh defaults this to the repository's examples/agent, which is the worked
# example: it loads and runs anywhere. The profile that runs THIS repository's own bot is the
# top-level playbooks/ directory, and an operator running that bot names it here — together
# with PODIUM_AGENT_SKILLS_DIR below, because its `podium` playbook names a skill and a
# playbook whose skill is missing fails its turns:
#
#   PODIUM_AGENT_PROFILE_DIR=/srv/podium/playbooks
#   PODIUM_AGENT_SKILLS_DIR=/srv/podium/skills
#
#PODIUM_AGENT_PROFILE_DIR=/etc/podium/agent

# Where third-party Agent Skills live on THIS host: one directory per skill, each holding a
# SKILL.md. There is no default, and it is not required — the Skills screen in the web UI stores
# skills in the conductor's own database, which is the way in that needs no shell here.
#
# This directory is the OTHER half of the library, and it outranks the browser: a name it holds
# cannot be replaced, disabled or deleted through the API, and it wins a clash. It is the escape
# hatch for when the UI or the database is unavailable. Unset means the stored half is the only
# source; with neither, a playbook that names a skill fails its turns saying so.
#
# A skill is executable content somebody else wrote, and it runs in the turn's container with
# that turn's GitHub token and model credential. Whoever can write this directory — and whoever
# can reach the web UI — decides what that is. Read docs/security.md before either.
#
# The repository's own skills are in its top-level skills/ directory, beside the playbooks/
# profile that names them, so they arrive by pull request rather than through the UI.
#PODIUM_AGENT_SKILLS_DIR=/etc/podium/agent/skills

# THE ASSISTANT. Where the built agent runtime lives on THIS host, and the podium-runner
# binary beside it. Set both and every conversation is answered by this process — the same
# runtime, the same brief, as a child of podium-agent instead of a container — and the work
# that needs a container (a repository, a Docker daemon, a browser) is DELEGATED to a task
# from there. Leave them unset and every turn is a task, including a chat message, which is
# what a host with no Node and no `opencode` on its PATH must do.
#
# The assistant is described by profile.yaml itself: its prompt, its model, its `skills:` and
# its `max_turns:`. It runs no playbook — the playbooks are what it delegates to.
#
# What it costs: no container around it. It runs as the user podium-agent runs as, on the
# machine that holds the master key and the provider credential, so it is given a short tool
# list (no shell, no filesystem, no repositories), a HOME of its own and an environment built
# from empty rather than inherited. Read docs/security.md before setting these.
#
# Build the runtime with `make agent-runtime-dist`; dist/ is build output and is not
# committed. Absolute paths, or the literal `auto` for "this checkout's own build" — which is
# what deploy/run-host.sh resolves, and the one to use when the checkout can move: a worktree's
# absolute path in a shared .env stops being true the day the worktree is deleted.
#
# `auto` that resolves to nothing is a startup ERROR and not a fall-back to container turns.
# Losing the assistant quietly is the failure worth refusing: the stack would come up, answer
# every chat, and pay for a container per message.
#
#   PODIUM_AGENT_HOST_RUNTIME=auto     # or /srv/podium/agent/runtime/dist/main.js
#   PODIUM_AGENT_RUNNER_BIN=auto       # or /srv/podium/bin/podium-runner
#
#PODIUM_AGENT_HOST_RUNTIME=
#PODIUM_AGENT_RUNNER_BIN=

# How many turns this host answers AT ONCE. Default 4.
#
# A host turn is a `node` process on this machine, so the ceiling is this host's rather than
# the fleet's. It mattered less when only the web chat was answered here — that is bounded by
# people at keyboards — and matters now that a Slack thread is too, where a busy channel
# decides how many conversations exist.
#
# Beyond the cap a conversation waits for a slot with its placeholder already posted: what a
# human sees is `👀 working…` for longer rather than silence, and the wait spends no Slack
# reads and no model tokens. podium_agent_host_turns_queued_total counts how often it happens,
# which is the number to watch before raising this.
#PODIUM_AGENT_HOST_MAX_TURNS=4

# The node binary that runs the host runtime, and where an assistant turn's own HOME, working
# directory and event socket are made. The defaults are `node` from PATH and the OS temp
# directory; set the second one to keep a turn's scratch off /tmp.
#PODIUM_AGENT_HOST_NODE=node
#PODIUM_AGENT_HOST_DIR=/var/lib/podium/agent/turns

# The Slack app-level token (xapp-…, scope connections:write) and bot token (xoxb-…). Both
# or neither: one alone is a startup error. Without them no Slack source is started and the
# conductor has nothing to listen to. As sensitive as any other credential here.
# deploy/slack-app-manifest.yaml is the app they come from.
#PODIUM_AGENT_SLACK_APP_TOKEN=
#PODIUM_AGENT_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# TEST ONLY. Mounts an in-process source and two routes (POST /dev/inbound, GET
# /dev/outbound) that inject messages as if a human had sent them and report everything the
# bot says. The startup log shouts when it is on. Never set it outside a test.
#PODIUM_AGENT_DEV_SOURCE=false

# --- shared memory (Hindsight) -------------------------------------------------------------
# One memory bank, shared by every agent turn, kept on this host. Memory is OPTIONAL: leave
# PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_URL empty and turns run without one — briefs carry no memory block,
# nothing is retained, and the UI's Memory tab says so. See docs/agent.md#memory.

# Where the CONDUCTOR reaches the memory service. http://hindsight:8888 in compose,
# http://127.0.0.1:8888 for a binary on a laptop. Setting it REQUIRES
# PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_API_KEY below.
#PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_URL=

# Where a TASK CONTAINER reaches the same service, which is a different vantage point: a
# turn runs on a node, on its own bridge network, and gets to the host through the bridge
# gateway. The default is right when the node runs on the same host as the memory service.
# With workers on other machines it has to be an address every node can route to — the
# host's tailnet IP, with PODIUM_MEMORY_BIND set to that IP. See docs/networking.md.
#PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_TASK_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8888

# The bank. One bank, everyone sees everything: there is no per-user or per-playbook scoping in
# this track. It becomes a path segment in the MCP URL every turn is handed.
#PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_BANK=podium

# REQUIRED when PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_URL is set — the conductor refuses to start without it,
# and the `hindsight` container refuses to start saying
# `HINDSIGHT_API_TENANT_API_KEY is required when using ApiKeyTenantExtension`.
# The memory service ships with NO AUTHENTICATION until it is given a key, so an install without one is a memory anybody who
# can reach port 8888 can read and rewrite. This one value has three consumers: the memory
# container (as HINDSIGHT_API_TENANT_API_KEY), this conductor, and every turn — the
# conductor copies it into the Podium secret podium.agent.memory_api_key at startup so each
# task container receives it as an ordinary secret. It is full read/write of every memory:
# as sensitive as PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN.
#PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_API_KEY=

# --- Linear -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Assign an issue to the bot user and it picks the work up; comment on that issue and it
# takes another turn. Linear is POLLED, never listened to: nothing on this host accepts an
# inbound connection it did not already accept. See docs/agent.md#linear.

# The bot user's PERSONAL API key (Settings -> Security & access -> Personal API keys, signed
# in AS THE BOT USER, not as you). Empty means no Linear source is started. A key that is set
# but does not work makes podium-agent EXIT at boot naming Linear, rather than silently never
# picking a ticket up. It is full read/write of everything that user can see: as sensitive as
# PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN. Exactly one playbooks/*.yaml must set `linear: true` for tickets to have
# something to run in.
#PODIUM_AGENT_LINEAR_API_KEY=

# How often assigned issues are asked for. Floor 10s. One query per tick against a budget of
# 2,500 requests an hour, so the default is two orders of magnitude inside it; that budget is
# shared by every API key the bot user owns.
#PODIUM_AGENT_LINEAR_POLL_INTERVAL=30s

# Linear's GraphQL endpoint. It is a test seam (the suite points it at a stub) and the hook
# for an egress proxy — it is NOT a "which Linear" knob.
#PODIUM_AGENT_LINEAR_URL=https://api.linear.app/graphql

# The Podium web UI as a HUMAN reaches it, which is not always how the conductor reaches the
# API: a tailnet name, a reverse proxy, a laptop tunnel. Default PODIUM_AGENT_SERVER. It is
# used for exactly one thing — the link a Linear comment falls back to when a screenshot
# cannot be uploaded — and the reader has to be signed in to Podium to open it.
#PODIUM_AGENT_UI_URL=

# Where an Anthropic key is validated before it is stored: GET <this>/v1/models with the
# pasted key. It is a test seam (the suite points it at a fake) and the hook for an egress
# proxy — it is NOT a BYOK knob. The provider is still Anthropic, and the conductor is the
# only thing that reads it: the model itself is called from inside a task container.
#PODIUM_AGENT_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com

# --- Grok ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A playbook can run on the `grok` agent backend instead of `claude`. It is the same runtime
# image and the same Claude Agent SDK, pointed at xAI's Anthropic-compatible /v1/messages —
# so nothing below installs anything, and a control plane that never sets a Grok credential
# simply reports the backend as "no credential" in the picker.
#
# The credential itself is NOT set here. It is pasted, or signed in for, on the web UI's
# Agent → Settings tab and stored as the Podium secret podium.agent.xai_api_key.

# Where an xAI credential is validated, and where a Grok turn's container sends the agent
# SDK's requests. Unlike the Anthropic one this is read twice: by the conductor to validate,
# and by every Grok turn. Point it at an egress proxy if this host has one.
#PODIUM_AGENT_XAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai

# The OIDC issuer a subscription sign-in (SuperGrok, X Premium+) discovers its endpoints
# from. The endpoints are never hard-coded: they come from
# <this>/.well-known/openid-configuration and are checked back against this host before any
# credential is sent to them.
#PODIUM_AGENT_XAI_OAUTH_ISSUER=https://auth.x.ai

# The OAuth client id of a public desktop client. It is public client metadata and NOT a
# secret — a public client cannot hold one, which is the whole reason the flow uses PKCE —
# so it is in this file and logged like any other field.
#
# EMPTY is the default, and it turns the subscription sign-in off and leaves the API key
# path. There is no client id to register: xAI operates no self-service registration, and
# every third-party tool that does this reuses **xAI's own Grok CLI client**. Hermes ships
# b1a00492-073a-47ea-816f-4c329264a828 hard-coded in hermes_cli/auth_constants.py, and the
# other implementations use the same one.
#
# Podium does not ship it as a default, for one reason worth knowing before you paste it in:
# the consent screen a human approves will name **xAI's CLI**, not Podium, because that is
# whose client id is being presented. Setting it is a decision about how your bot introduces
# itself to your own identity provider, so it is left to you.
#PODIUM_AGENT_XAI_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=

# What a sign-in asks for. offline_access is the one that matters: without it xAI issues no
# refresh token, and a human signs in again every time the access token expires.
#PODIUM_AGENT_XAI_OAUTH_SCOPES=openid profile email offline_access grok-cli:access api:access

# --- the server-side half of the reverse proxy ---------------------------------------------
# These two are read by podium-server, not by the conductor, and they are what lets a browser
# reach the AgentService at all: the server forwards /podium.agent.v1.AgentService/ to
# PODIUM_AGENT_URL behind its own identity middleware, adding the bearer below and an
# X-Podium-Login header naming the operator (any client-supplied copy is stripped).

# Where the conductor listens, as podium-server reaches it: scheme://host:port with NO path.
# Unset means this control plane has no conductor — the prefix is not mounted, WhoAmI reports
# agent_enabled=false and the web UI hides its Agent screen. Setting it REQUIRES
# PODIUM_AGENT_TOKEN: a proxy that forwards an unauthenticated request into the conductor is
# worse than no proxy. In compose this is http://agent:8090.
#PODIUM_AGENT_URL=

# PODIUM_AGENT_TOKEN above is shared by both sides: podium-server presents it and the
# conductor requires it. Set it once.

# =============================================================================
# podium — the CLI
# =============================================================================
# Both are overridden by --server and --token, and both override
# ~/.config/podium/config.yaml.

#PODIUM_SERVER=http://127.0.0.1:8080

# The token the CLI presents. Rarely set: under the local transport the CLI already reads
# PODIUM_LOCAL_TOKEN above, and a tailnet control plane needs no token at all — Tailscale's
# WhoIs names the caller, so `podium --server https://podium.<tailnet>.ts.net nodes` just
# works. Set this only to point the CLI at a stack whose token is not the one in this file.
#PODIUM_TOKEN=

# =============================================================================
# Read by the compose files only — no Podium binary looks at these
# =============================================================================

# REQUIRED by docker-compose.yml and docker-compose.tailnet.yml. The Postgres password;
# the compose file builds PODIUM_DATABASE_URL from it. docker-compose.dev.yml and
# run-host.sh both fall back to `podium` when it is empty, so a copied .env works as-is
# for development and only a real deployment has to fill it in.
PODIUM_PG_PASSWORD=

# Where the compose files pull Podium's own four images from. The default is the published
# location; set it to a registry of your own if you mirror them, which an air-gapped or
# pull-through deployment has to. The four repository names under it are fixed:
# podium-server, podium-node, podium-agent and podium.
#PODIUM_IMAGE_REPO=ghcr.io/podium-ade

# The four image references, whole, each overriding PODIUM_IMAGE_REPO and PODIUM_IMAGE_TAG for
# one service. These exist for one reason: a tag is mutable and a digest is not, and
# `repo/name:tag` cannot express a digest — `name@sha256:…` uses a different separator, so no
# value of PODIUM_IMAGE_TAG can produce one. A production deployment pins digests:
#
#   PODIUM_SERVER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/podium-ade/podium-server@sha256:...
#
# Resolve them from a release with `docker buildx imagetools inspect <image>:<tag>`.
#PODIUM_SERVER_IMAGE=
#PODIUM_AGENT_IMAGE=
#PODIUM_NODE_IMAGE=
#PODIUM_CLI_IMAGE=

# Which released image tag the compose files pull for podium-server and podium-node. Pin it
# to a version you have tested: `latest` moves under you, and a control plane and its
# workers from different releases can disagree about the wire (`podium version` warns).
#PODIUM_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# Where the server keeps its state: the master key `init` generates, and the tsnet device
# identity under the tailnet transport. A bare name is a Docker volume; a PATH is a bind mount,
# and compose tells them apart by the leading dot or slash. Setting it to a path is worth
# considering: the master key is then a file you can see and back up, and `docker compose
# down -v` cannot destroy it. It is written mode 0600 either way, because the server refuses a
# key file any other account on the machine can read. Who owns it differs: on a native Linux
# engine it stays uid 65532, so reading it from the host takes sudo; Docker Desktop maps the
# ownership to you, so it does not.
#PODIUM_STATE_DIR=server-state

# Which host port docker-compose.yml publishes the server on, loopback only.
#PODIUM_PORT=8080

# Which host port docker-compose.yml publishes the memory service on. Unlike the others this
# one is NOT loopback-only — see PODIUM_MEMORY_BIND for why, and firewall it. Change the port
# when something else already owns 8888.
#PODIUM_MEMORY_PORT=8888

# Which host port docker-compose.dev.yml publishes Postgres on, loopback only. Change it
# when something else already owns 5432, and match PODIUM_DATABASE_URL to it.
#PODIUM_PG_PORT=5432

# REQUIRED by docker-compose.tailnet.yml. Your tailnet's MagicDNS suffix WITHOUT .ts.net —
# `tailscale status --json` reports it as MagicDNSSuffix. The node service dials
# https://podium.<this>.ts.net.
#PODIUM_TAILNET=

# --- the memory container ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by the `hindsight` service in the compose files. PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_API_KEY above is
# the other half: it is what guards the service, and it is shared with the conductor.

# REQUIRED by the `hindsight` service — but not by compose interpolation, so a stack with no
# memory still comes up. Left empty, the `hindsight` container refuses to start and says
# `LLM API key is required`. The LLM key the memory service extracts facts with — any of
# Hindsight's ~25 providers, chosen with PODIUM_MEMORY_LLM_PROVIDER below, not Anthropic's in
# particular. It is read as an environment variable at container start, so it comes from here
# rather than from the web UI's secret store — and its calls are billed like any other. It
# may be the same key the agents use. There is no `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in that container:
# the variable is HINDSIGHT_API_LLM_API_KEY, and the compose file builds it from this.
#
# GET THIS WRONG AND NOTHING SAYS SO. A key the model API rejects does not stop the memory
# service starting and does not fail a turn: every retain is still accepted, /health stays
# green, the conductor logs a hand-off for each one — and extraction 401s in the background,
# retries, gives up, and writes no fact. The bank simply stays empty. Watch
# podium_agent_memory_extraction_failed on the conductor's /metrics, which is non-zero for
# exactly this, and see docs/agent.md#memory.
#PODIUM_MEMORY_LLM_API_KEY=

# Which provider that key belongs to. Hindsight puts LiteLLM underneath and accepts around
# 25 of them — openai, anthropic, gemini, groq, bedrock, vertexai, deepseek, litellm, an
# existing ChatGPT or Claude subscription, or a local ollama / lmstudio / llamacpp, which
# keeps extraction off the network entirely. The list and what each one wants:
# https://hindsight.vectorize.io/developer/models
#PODIUM_MEMORY_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic

# Which model does that extraction. Must match the provider above. It is background work over
# short prose, so a cheaper model is a reasonable choice — Podium does not make it for you.
#PODIUM_MEMORY_LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Which interface docker-compose.yml publishes the memory port on. LOOPBACK by default, so
# that PODIUM_AGENT_MEMORY_API_KEY having a default cannot expose the bank: Hindsight has no
# authentication beyond that key. A worker running agent turns needs it wider — a turn's
# container reaches the host through the bridge gateway, which a service on 127.0.0.1 is not
# reachable from — so set this to the bridge-gateway or tailnet address and set a real key at
# the same time. docs/security.md says which ranges to firewall it to.
# Set it to the tailnet IP or the bridge gateway IP for something tighter. The dev compose
# file defaults it to 127.0.0.1 instead.
#PODIUM_MEMORY_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Which host port docker-compose.dev.yml publishes the memory service on.
#PODIUM_MEMORY_PORT=8888

# =============================================================================
# Read by deploy/run-host.sh only — the `make stack-up` path
# =============================================================================
# run-host.sh runs the four binaries on this host from this file, instead of as containers.
# It is what `make stack-up` calls, and the only supported way to run the conductor under
# PODIUM_TRANSPORT=tailnet — see the note at the top of that script.

# Where the built binaries are. `make build` writes them to ./bin.
#PODIUM_BIN_DIR=bin

# Where pid files and logs go. master.key is read from beside THIS file, not from here.
#PODIUM_STATE_DIR=.podium

# Extra arguments for podium-node only, word-split. This is where a local worker is given
# --allow-privileged-sidecars, which no environment variable turns on for a host binary.
#PODIUM_NODE_ARGS=

# Which file run-host.sh reads. The one value that cannot come from the file, because it
# names it. Pass it in the environment: PODIUM_ENV_FILE=… make stack-up
#PODIUM_ENV_FILE=deploy/.env

# =============================================================================
# Read by deploy/install-node.sh only — passed on its command line, not from here
# =============================================================================
# The installer turns a Linux machine into a worker. It runs before there is a Podium on
# that machine to read a .env, so it takes these in its own environment:
#
#   curl -fsSL .../install-node.sh | sudo PODIUM_SERVER=… PODIUM_ENROLL_TOKEN=… bash
#
# THE NAMES ARE NOT THE DAEMON'S. The installer writes /etc/podium/node.yaml, so it takes
# PODIUM_LABELS and writes `labels:`, where the daemon itself reads PODIUM_NODE_LABELS.
# Listed here so the whole deployment surface is in one file, but do not set them here.

# The enrollment token from `podium node enroll-token`. Becomes PODIUM_NODE_ENROLL_TOKEN.
#PODIUM_ENROLL_TOKEN=

# Comma-separated labels for the worker. Becomes the daemon's PODIUM_NODE_LABELS.
#PODIUM_LABELS=

# How many tasks at once. Becomes the daemon's PODIUM_NODE_MAX_TASKS.
#PODIUM_MAX_TASKS=4

# The worker's Prometheus listener. Becomes the daemon's PODIUM_NODE_METRICS_LISTEN.
#PODIUM_METRICS_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:9091

# Where the installer puts things. Change these only to install somewhere unusual.
#PODIUM_CONFIG_DIR=/etc/podium
#PODIUM_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/podium-node
#PODIUM_UNIT_DIR=/etc/systemd/system

# How long it waits for the node to report itself online before giving up.
#PODIUM_WAIT_SECONDS=90

# Which release to install, and where from. PODIUM_VERSION defaults to the latest release;
# the three URLs exist so the installer can be tested against a local release server.
#PODIUM_VERSION=
#PODIUM_REPO=podium-ade/podium
#PODIUM_RELEASE_BASE_URL=https://github.com/podium-ade/podium/releases/download
#PODIUM_RAW_BASE_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/podium-ade/podium
